{"advice":"Experiment: add an n=512 full-tile SME2 path to `existing-sme` (source hash `3719c58530161598ab9a537a43be749a5287c8d8dbd35b855e5afea97cd15258`) that replaces the four separate `svld1_f32` calls in each reduction step with two `svld1_f32_x2` paired loads, one for adjacent A vectors and one for adjacent B vectors. Extract the two vectors from each pair and keep the same four `svmopa_za32_f32_m` calls, selectors 0–3, predicates, increasing-k order, ZA stores, and general-size fallback. The compiler’s current block uses four individual `ld1w` instructions before four MOPAs; the supplied SME2 probe shows paired loads compile to two multi-vector `ld1w` instructions. Hypothesis: reducing load instruction count may improve the full-tile reduction loop. This is unverified; the probe provides code-generation and correctness evidence only, not GEMM timing. Inspect candidate assembly to confirm paired loads are emitted in the hot loop and no new spills appear. Check n=512 and general sizes, odd tails, guards, and tolerance 0.002. Then compare repeated fresh AC scores with unchanged paired controls under the fixed gates.","risks":"Paired loads may add extraction or register pressure, be equivalent after compiler optimization, or fail to improve end-to-end GEMM. Full-vector paired loads require guarded handling where tiles are partial; keep them confined to full n=512 tiles and use the existing path elsewhere. Do not infer a gain from instruction count or noisy scores.","abstain":false}
